  <p>
    <strong>This is NOT survivalism.</strong> This is engineering capability — the ability to maintain your
    chosen civilization level regardless of supply chain status. Cuba, Venezuela, rural Africa, and post-Soviet
    states have all demonstrated that communities survive disruption through fabrication capability, not
    stockpiles.
  </p>

  <h3>Dave Gingery's Bootstrap Sequence</h3>
  <p>
    David J. Gingery (1932-2004) wrote a seven-book series proving you can build a complete machine shop from
    literal scrap metal. The sequence is a build chain where each machine enables the next:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Book 1: The Charcoal Foundry</strong> ($0&ndash;$500 in scrap) — Melts aluminum from automotive parts using a firebrick foundry and a blower salvaged from a clothes dryer.</li>
    <li><strong>Book 2: The Metal Lathe</strong> — Cast from the foundry. The lathe refines its own parts — it literally helps build itself.</li>
    <li><strong>Book 3: The Metal Shaper</strong> — Built using lathe and foundry. Produces flat surfaces the lathe cannot.</li>
    <li><strong>Book 4: The Milling Machine</strong> — Built using all prior machines. Precision surfaces, slots, keyways.</li>
    <li><strong>Book 5: The Drill Press</strong> — Precision hole-making beyond hand drill capability.</li>
    <li><strong>Book 6: The Dividing Head</strong> — Enables cutting gears and splines. Turns a workshop into one that can build complex mechanisms.</li>
    <li><strong>Book 7: The Sheet Metal Brake</strong> — Bending and forming for enclosures, guards, and structural components.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Total cost: approximately $50 for the book set. Raw materials: effectively free (scrap metal + charcoal).
    The result: a complete machine shop capable of making precision metal parts, including parts for more tools.
  </p>

  <h3>Precious Plastic: Open-Source Recycling</h3>
  <p>
    <a href="https://www.preciousplastic.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Precious Plastic</a> by
    Dave Hakkens provides open-source designs for four machines that create a closed-loop plastic recycling system:
    <strong>shredder</strong> (grinds waste into flakes), <strong>extrusion machine</strong> (melts flakes into
    continuous profiles and filament), <strong>injection molder</strong> (specific parts from molds), and
    <strong>sheet press</strong> (flat sheets from compressed flakes). All designs are downloadable. DIY build cost:
    $200&ndash;$2,000 per machine. Combined with 3D printing, this turns plastic waste into raw material for
    tools, water pipe, containers, greenhouse panels, and replacement parts.
  </p>

  <h3>The Scavenger-Ready Philosophy</h3>
  <p>
    In any disruption scenario — economic collapse, supply chain failure, natural disaster, political isolation
    — a community's survival depends on its ability to: <strong>salvage</strong> useful components from broken
    technology (stepper motors from printers, alternators from vehicles, copper from motors, neodymium magnets
    from hard drives), <strong>process</strong> scavenged materials into usable forms (melt aluminum in a
    charcoal foundry, sort steel by spark test, strip copper wire, shred and extrude plastics), and
    <strong>fabricate</strong> replacement parts and new tools from processed materials.
  </p>
  <p>
    The key capability chain:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Scavenged metal</strong> &rarr; Foundry/Forge &rarr; Cast/forged parts &rarr; Machines</li>
    <li><strong>Scavenged plastic</strong> &rarr; Shredder &rarr; Recycled flake &rarr; Extruded/molded products</li>
    <li><strong>Scavenged electronics</strong> &rarr; Disassembly &rarr; Components &rarr; New circuits/generators</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="cta-box">
    <p class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800 mb-2">The library is the most important infrastructure</p>
    <p>Tools without knowledge are scrap metal. A curated collection of technical books and reference materials — stored digitally on solar-powered devices and as physical books that require no electricity — is the single highest-value investment for long-term community resilience.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="bar-section">
  <h2>Self-Replicating Workshop</h2>
  <p>
    Beyond processing equipment and fabrication tools, the Techno Park aims to build something more fundamental:
    a self-replicating workshop — a set of tools that can reproduce and repair themselves. This is not science
    fiction. Dave Gingery's seven-book series demonstrates how to build a complete machine shop starting from
    nothing but scrap metal and a charcoal foundry. The sequence begins with casting a lathe bed from aluminum
    cans, then using that lathe to build a shaper, then a milling machine, then a drill press, and so on. Total
    cost for the starting foundry: $0&ndash;$500 in scrap materials. The result: a workshop capable of making
    precision metal parts — including parts for more tools.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <a href="https://reprap.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">RepRap project</a> took this idea into the digital domain. RepRap is a self-replicating 3D printer — a
    machine that can print the majority of its own components. The project has achieved approximately <a href="https://reprap.org/wiki/RepRap" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">50&ndash;70% self-replication by component weight</a>,
    meaning a working RepRap can produce the majority of the parts needed to build another RepRap.
    Combined with the Gingery approach for metal components, this creates a bootstrapping path from scrap
    materials to a fully equipped fabrication shop.
  </p>
  <p>
    Open Source Ecology's Global Village Construction Set (GVCS) scales this concept to civilization level:
    50 open-source machines — from tractors to circuit makers — designed so that communities can build and
    maintain their own industrial infrastructure. All designs are freely available, and several machines are
    directly buildable from materials available in the Yungas.
  </p>
  <p>
    The "scavenger-ready" concept ties these threads together. In a region where Amazon deliveries don't arrive
    and replacement parts take weeks by bus, the ability to salvage components from broken electronics, vehicles,
    and machinery — and fabricate replacements — is not a hobby, it's a survival skill. Precious Plastic, an
    open-source project providing designs for plastic recycling machines (shredder, extruder, injection molder,
    sheet press), adds another dimension: turning plastic waste into raw material for new parts and products.
  </p>
  <p>
    Minimum Viable Workshop cost: $700&ndash;$3,000. That gets you a Gingery-style foundry, a basic RepRap
    printer, hand tools, and the knowledge base to start building everything else. The goal is not to buy a
    workshop — it's to grow one.
  </p>
  </div>

